      "text": "To determine the range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\), we need to analyze the function by considering different cases based on the critical points where the expressions inside the absolute values change sign. These critical points are \\( x = -1 \\) and \\( x = 1 \\). We will divide the real number line into three intervals: \\( (-\\infty, -1) \\), \\( [-1, 1] \\), and \\( (1, \\infty) \\).\n\n### Case 1: \\( x < -1 \\)\nIn this interval, both \\( x+1 \\) and \\( x-1 \\) are negative. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ |x+1| = -(x+1) = -x-1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x+1 \\]\nSubstituting these into the function, we get:\n\\[ G(x) = -x-1 - (-x+1) = -x-1 + x-1 = -2 \\]\nSo, for \\( x < -1 \\), \\( G(x) = -2 \\).\n\n### Case 2: \\( -1 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\)\nIn this interval, \\( x+1 \\) is non-negative and \\( x-1 \\) is non-positive. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ |x+1| = x+1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x+1 \\]\nSubstituting these into the function, we get:\n\\[ G(x) = x+1 - (-x+1) = x+1 + x-1 = 2x \\]\nSo, for \\( -1 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\), \\( G(x) = 2x \\). As \\( x \\) ranges from \\( -1 \\) to \\( 1 \\), \\( G(x) \\) ranges from \\( 2(-1) = -2 \\) to \\( 2(1) = 2 \\). Therefore, for \\( -1 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\), \\( G(x) \\) ranges from \\( -2 \\) to \\( 2 \\).\n\n### Case 3: \\( x > 1 \\)\nIn this interval, both \\( x+1 \\) and \\( x-1 \\) are positive. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ |x+1| = x+1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = x-1 \\]\nSubstituting these into the function, we get:\n\\[ G(x) = x+1 - (x-1) = x+1 - x+1 = 2 \\]\nSo, for \\( x > 1 \\), \\( G(x) = 2 \\).\n\n### Summary\nFrom the above cases, we have:\n- For \\( x < -1 \\), \\( G(x) = -2 \\).\n- For \\( -1 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\), \\( G(x) \\) ranges from \\( -2 \\) to \\( 2 \\).\n- For \\( x > 1 \\), \\( G(x) = 2 \\).\n\nCombining these, the range of \\( G(x) \\) is all values from \\( -2 \\) to \\( 2 \\), inclusive. Therefore, the range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{[-2, 2]} \\]",
