      "text": "The complete problem and the given solution are in\"math long answer\" format. We need to decide whether or not to change the format.\n\nThe function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\) can be broken down based on the points where the expressions inside the absolute values change sign, which are \\( x = -1 \\) and \\( x = 1 \\). We will analyze the function in the intervals determined by these points: \\( (-\\infty, -1) \\), \\( [-1, 1] \\), and \\( (1, \\infty) \\).\n\n**Interval 1: \\( (-\\infty, -1) \\)**\nIn this interval, \\( x + 1 < 0 \\) and \\( x - 1 < 0 \\), so:\n\\[ |x+1| = -(x+1) = -x-1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x+1 \\]\nThus,\n\\[ G(x) = -x-1 - (-x+1) = -x-1 + x - 1 = -2 \\]\nSo, for all \\( x \\) in \\( (-\\infty, -1) \\),\n\\[ G(x) = -2 \\]\n\n**Interval 2: \\( [-1, 1] \\)**\nIn this interval, \\( x + 1 \\ge 0 \\) and \\( x - 1 \\le 0 \\), so:\n\\[ |x+1| = x+1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x+1 \\]\nThus,\n\\[ G(x) = x+1 - (-x+1) = x+1 + x - 1 = 2x \\]\nSo, for all \\( x \\) in \\( [-1, 1] \\),\n\\[ G(x) = 2x \\]\nAt the endpoints \\( x = -1 \\) and \\( x = 1 \\), we have:\n\\[ G(-1) = 2(-1) = -2 \\]\n\\[ G(1) = 2(1) = 2 \\]\n\n**Interval 3: \\( (1, \\infty) \\)**\nIn this interval, \\( x + 1 > 0 \\) and \\( x - 1 > 0 \\), so:\n\\[ |x+1| = x+1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = x-1 \\]\nThus,\n\\[ G(x) = x+1 - (x-1) = x+1 - x + 1 = 2 \\]\nSo, for all \\( x \\) in \\( (1, \\infty) \\),\n\\[ G(x) = 2 \\]\n\nCombining these results, we see that \\( G(x) = -2 \\) for \\( x \\in (-\\infty, -1) \\), \\( G(x) = 2x \\) for \\( x \\in [-1, 1] \\), and \\( G(x) = 2 \\) for \\( x \\in (1, \\infty) \\). Therefore, the range of \\( G(x) \\) is all real numbers from the minimum value to the maximum value in the intervals, which is from \\( -2 \\) to \\( 2 \\). Thus, the range of \\( G(x) \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{[-2, 2]} \\]\n\nLet's verify this with a Python code to ensure accuracy by evaluating the function over a range of \\( x \\) values.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\r\n\r\n# Define the function G(x)\r\ndef G(x):\r\n    return np.abs(x + 1) - np.abs(x - 1)\r\n\r\n# Generate a large range of x values to check the function's output\r\nx_values = np.linspace(-10, 10, 10000)\r\nG_values = np.array([G(x) for x in x_values])\r\n\r\n# Determine the minimum and maximum values of G(x)\r\nG_min = np.min(G_values)\r\nG_max = np.max(G_values)\r\n\r\nprint(((G_min, G_max)))\n```",
